                    <physDesc>
                        <handDesc hands="5">
                            <!-- https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/56/article/754633/pdf -->
                            <handNote xml:id="scribe1">
                                <p>The [h] character in Scribe 1 is distinguished by a sharp angle
                                    at the top of the first vertical as the quill changes direction,
                                    a bowed crossbar, a round loop, and a very slight foot at the
                                    base of the second vertical. The word-end [m] and [n] glyphs
                                    conclude with a backward flourish that stretches as far as the
                                    penultimate minim.</p>
                            </handNote>
                            <handNote xml:id="scribe2">
                                <p>more cramped than Scribe 1, with a slightly slanted character.
                                    This scribe uses a horizontal, straight crossbar, an oval loop,
                                    and an upwardly angled final tick. The final backstroke of [m]
                                    and [n] is short, barely passing the final minim.</p>
                            </handNote>
                            <handNote xml:id="scribe3">
                                <p>The [h] written by Scribe 3 is similar to that of Scribe 1,
                                    although slightly more compact. The final stroke of [m] and [n]
                                    curves back on itself, nearly touching the top of the final
                                    minim.</p>
                            </handNote>
                            <handNote xml:id="scribe4">
                                <p>The [h] written by Scribe 4 has a perpendicular crossbar, an
                                    oversize loop, and a prominent final foot. The final stroke of
                                    [m] and [n] is tall, with only a slight curvature.</p>
                            </handNote>
                            <handNote xml:id="scribe5">
                                <p>the [h] is tall and narrow, with a bowed cross-stroke that begins
                                    at the top of the vertical, and a minuscule tick at the foot of
                                    the second vertical. The [m] has a long, low finial that
                                    finishes above the penultimate minim</p>
                            </handNote>
                        </handDesc>
                        <bindingDesc>
                            <p><material>Goat</material> Skin</p>
                        </bindingDesc>
                    </physDesc>
                    <history>
                        <origin notBefore="1404" notAfter="1438">
                            <!-- https://www.voynich.nu/extra/carbon.html -->
                            <p>The origins of this manuscript are unknown, but it has been carbon
                                dated to have been made sometime between <date notBefore="1404"
                                    notAfter="1438">1404 and 1438</date>.</p>
                        </origin>
                        <provenance>
                            <!-- https://www.voynich.nu/curricula.html -->
                            <p>Any history between <date notBefore="1404" notAfter="1576">1404 and
                                    1576</date> is missing and unknown.</p>
                        </provenance>
                        <provenance>
                            <p>Owned by Rudolph II of Habsburg during his reign in Prague from <date
                                    notBefore="1576" notAfter="1611">1576 to 1611</date>. In <date
                                    when="1599">1599</date>, Karl Widemann sold Rudolph II several
                                books, one of which was likely the Voynich Manuscript, but this is
                                not confirmed..</p>
                        </provenance>
                        <provenance>
                            <p>Jacobus Horcicky de Tepenecz owned the manuscript in <date
                                    when="1622">1622</date>. This is noted in an inscription on
                                folio 1r which reads <q>Jacobi de Tepenecz</q> under uv light. The
                                manuscript was likely owned by someone else and given to him as a
                                part of his inheritance.</p>
                        </provenance>
                        <provenance>
                            <p>Owned by Georgius Barschius potentially sometime between <date
                                    notBefore="1627" notAfter="1637">1627 and 1637</date>.</p>
                        </provenance>
                        <provenance>
                            <p>Johannes Marcus Marci of Kronland was gifted Barschius' alchemical
                                library in <date when="1677">1677</date>, which included the Voynich
                                Manuscript.</p>
                        </provenance>
                        <provenance>
                            <p>Wilfred M. Voynich acquired the manuscript in <date when="1912"
                                    >1912</date> at the Villa Mondragone in Italy</p>
                        </provenance>
                        <provenance>
                            <!-- https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/collections/highlights/voynich-manuscript -->
                            <p>H. P. Kraus purchases the manuscript from Ethel Voynich, Wilfrid
                                Voynich's widow.</p>
                        </provenance>
                        <provenance>
                            <p>Given to the Beinecke Library by H. P. Kraus</p>
                        </provenance>
                    </history>
                </msDesc>
            </sourceDesc>
